diagnosis_text:
The Office B client segment is up and routing correctly; no network-side fault is present. From the affected endpoint (PC3 on CD_CLIENT_B2), basic L2/L3 functions are confirmed: the NIC is UP with 10.3.0.100/24, and its default route points to 10.3.0.1. PC3 successfully pings its default gateway 10.3.0.1 with 0% loss, and the ARP table shows valid neighbor entries (10.3.0.1/10.3.0.2 MAC learned), proving the local VLAN and switch uplink are intact. Beyond the local segment, PC3 reaches other offices and services: pc1 (10.1.0.100) and pc2 (10.1.0.101) both reply with 0% loss, and srv1 (10.5.0.4) in Office B’s server VLAN responds with 0% loss (TTL=63), showing inter-VLAN routing through the distribution/core is working.

If the Office B segment were disconnected, PC3 would fail to ARP the gateway and would not be able to reach remote subnets; our tests show the opposite. Localization: PC3 on CD_CLIENT_B2 via access switch aswb2 towards the B distribution pair is operating normally; no anomalies observed on that path.

Root cause: No network break or misconfiguration detected; the reported outage is not reproducible from PC3—likely a transient or endpoint-specific issue unrelated to the campus network.

Corrective fix: No network changes required. If the user still experiences issues, investigate the endpoint (check/replace patch cable, ensure NIC enabled, renew IP/DNS settings, and verify application/DNS resolution). 
